ere and not there  i don't even know what the use of  restoring everything is we fix it now  and tomorrow another shell hits  boris has evacuated his wife to western  ukraine where his daughter lives  you see what's happening here  nevertheless he's determined to stay as  long as help is needed  as the saying goes hope is the last one  to die i'm on ukraine's side  i trust the news and i was born in this  town  i hope there will be no incoming shell  sounds almost like we need to dive  just months ago they were dreaming of  creating a cultural hub in new york  now it's all about surviving  well dw correspondent rebecca ritters is  in keeve and earlier she told us ukraine  is struggling to hold back the russian  assault in donbass  well the situation in the donbass is  going very very badly for the ukrainians  at this stage pablo excuse me uh the  they've been admitting for a couple of  days now that they have lost the upper  hand there that russia really has the  upper hand and is dominating the battle  in luhansk and donetsk and as you heard  from that report that certainly seems to  be the case 95 percent of luhansk is now  under russian control  and they are  going forward as well the city of  several donetsk where most of the  fighting is now centered in that region  uh they the russian troops are now  encircling that city there is some mixed  reports as to whether or not the  russians have gained control of that  city russia saying that they have  ukraine saying that they haven't yet but  they have said for a couple of days now  that they may have to pull ukrainian  troops out of that area in able to to  shore up defenses further back they just  don't have the the heavy weaponry they  need to be able to hold back the  russians and push them back they're  they're calling uh on the international  community pleading with the  international community to send these  big weapons particularly multiple rocket  launchers particularly rather multiple  rocket launchers that can fire up to 300  kilometers in the distance they need  this kind of weaponry if they're going  to be able to stop  russia's advance but whether it comes in  time really remains to be seen pablo  well in his daily address ukrainian  president volodysilenski talked about  the human cost of war  how bad is the situation for the  civilians stuck in the areas under  bombardment  as we know civilians  always pay the highest cost in these  situations in these wars the people  living in that area as you heard from  some of them you know the situation is  really dire for civilians i mean  similarly to what we saw in places like  mariupol that were being completely  bombarded for weeks months  people have to live completely almost  underground they they don't have access  to fresh water they run out of food  you know they're living in darkness most  of the time they're being completely  pounded by russian and and also  ukrainian uh bombs in defense of the  regions as well so you know these  situations uh is just dire for the  civilians on the ground and it doesn't  look like it's getting better any time  soon  rebecca ritter is in keeve thank you  meanwhile russian president vladimir  putin has again spoken to german  chancellor olaf schultz and french  president emmanuel macron by telephone  he reportedly told them he's ready to  talk about lifting a blockade in the  black sea  that has hit ukrainian farmers hard and  is threatening a global food crisis  a bumper crop  usually that is good news for a farmer  but with exports out of ukraine's black  seaports blocked  these grain silos in dinipro are still  full from the last harvest  you can see that the field is in  excellent condition the harvest will be  good but where should we keep it we  don't know  road transport is a last resort  but it is too slow to empty the  granaries  the next harvest is due mid-july  if ports remain closed  this farmer says he faces bankruptcy  closer to the shifting front line  farming may be too dangerous  these fields near harkeef have been  reclaimed but they are still littered  with mines and shells  some farmers continue to seed the soil  with the limited equipment left  there are a lot of holes  the whole farmland is full of holes left  by shelling  we don't have strikes right now  but there are shells and rockets on the  ground  so we drive past them and still  seed what else can we do  russia has said it would be willing to  allow grain exports  if the west end sanctions  as food prices continue to rise sharply  the ongoing crisis is expected to push  millions more around the world into  hunger
 

